course: dictionary
lesson: 188
title: Aircraft and its plural
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Aircraft is a noun. It names any machine that flies through the air, such as an aeroplane, helicopter, glider or airship. When you meet a word like this, look at the sentence: «On board the aircraft, passengers fasten their seat belts» tells you people are inside a flying machine.
explain: The word has two parts, air and craft. Craft here means a machine that travels, so the clue points to flight. Some phrases use aircraft in a special way. An aircraft carrier is a huge ship that aeroplanes can take off from and land on. A ship does not fly, so the words around it show you this phrase names a ship.
explain: Aircraft is one of the nouns that stay the same in the plural. The example says «Some nouns are the same in the plural: sheep, deer, moose, aircraft, and usually fish.» So you say one aircraft and two aircraft, not two aircrafts. Check the word by swapping in your meaning: a machine that flies still makes sense.
fact: Aircraft means any machine that flies in the air.
fact: Examples of aircraft are aeroplanes, helicopters, gliders and airships.
fact: An aircraft carrier is a huge ship that aeroplanes can take off from and land on.
fact: «Aircraft» is the same in the plural: one aircraft, two aircraft.
fact: Other nouns that stay the same in the plural include sheep, deer and moose.
exercise: What is the plural of aircraft? | *Aircraft | One aircraft | Aircrafts | Aircrafties
exercise: Which of these is an aircraft? | A wooden chair | A railway track | A garden hose | *A glider
exercise: What is an aircraft carrier? | A flying machine that carries only letters | *A huge ship that aeroplanes can take off from and land on | A small boat for fishing in a river | A train that runs under the sea
test: Which of these is NOT an aircraft? | An airship | *A bicycle | A helicopter | A glider
test: What does «on board the aircraft» mean? | Outside the airport gates | *Inside the flying machine | Next to a lighthouse on the cliff | Under the water near the harbour
exam: Which sentence uses aircraft correctly? | Three aircraft-s were parked on the runway. | Three aircrafts were parked on the runway. | Three aircraftes were parked on the runway. | *Three aircraft were parked on the runway.
